drm/msm/dsi: report a mode clock that matches the mode's own timings

THE WHOLE DESKTOP RUNS AT 24 fps ON A 60 Hz PANEL WITHOUT THIS.

Measured on gts7fewifi (FT8203 WQXGA, 1600x2560) 2026-08-02. modetest:

    #0 1600x2560x60x112346vidNS 24.21 1600 1690 1698 1787 2560 2576 2584 2597 112346

Two numbers in that line disagree. The mode is NAMED 60 and drm_mode->vrefresh
is 60, but the advertised clock of 112346 kHz against the advertised 1787x2597
timings works out to 24.21 Hz, and 24.21 is what every DRM client believes.
wlroots logs it verbatim:

    Found connector 'DSI-1'
      1600x2560 @ 24.208 Hz (preferred)
    connector DSI-1: Modesetting with 1600x2560 @ 24.208 Hz

Phosh then schedules one frame every 41 ms on a panel that is really scanning
out every 16.7 ms. The device feels broken; dh called it "unusable laggy". No
amount of GPU work fixes it, because the compositor is deliberately idling.

WHY THE NUMBER IS WRONG

The panel uses DSC. For a compressed link the driver computes the pixel clock
over the COMPRESSED line width, dsi_panel.c:

    pixel_clk_khz = (dsi_h_total_dce(&mode->timing) *
                     DSI_V_TOTAL(&mode->timing) *
                     mode->timing.refresh_rate);
    do_div(pixel_clk_khz, 1000);

and dsi_h_total_dce() -> dsi_h_active_dce() returns dsc->pclk_per_line rather
than h_active whenever DSC is on (dsi_defs.h). That is CORRECT for programming
the link: the DSI clock carries compressed pixels. 112346 kHz over the
uncompressed 2597 vtotal implies ~721 h units against the mode's 1787, i.e.
the expected ~3:1 DSC ratio, so the value is exactly what the hardware needs.

The bug is exporting that link rate as drm_display_mode.clock, whose timings
are the UNCOMPRESSED 1787x2597. DRM defines a mode's refresh as

    refresh = clock * 1000 / (htotal * vtotal)

(drm_mode_vrefresh(); userspace open-codes the same). A clock belonging to a
different htotal makes that identity produce nonsense. The two fields must
describe the same picture, and only drm_mode->clock is free to change:
hdisplay/htotal are what the compositor must actually scan out.

THE FIX

Derive the exported clock from the timings exported beside it, so
drm_mode_vrefresh() returns refresh_rate by construction. dsi_mode->
pixel_clk_khz is untouched, so DSI/pclk programming is unaffected: this
changes only what is advertised to userspace.

Non-DSC panels keep the same value (h_total_dce == htotal there, so the old
and new expressions are equal), which is why this is not gated on dsc_enabled:
fewer paths, identical result.

VERIFY AFTER FLASHING

    modetest -M msm_drm -c | grep 1600x2560      # refresh ~60.00, clock ~278450
    grep "Modesetting with" ~/phosh-session.log  # "@ 60.0xx Hz", not 24.208

INCOMPLETE FIRST ATTEMPT (recorded so it is not repeated): changing only the
forward conversion boots to a DEAD DISPLAY. Two places match a drm mode back
to a dsi mode BY THAT CLOCK, so correcting it made the driver unable to find
its own mode:

    [drm:dsi_display_find_mode] *ERROR* failed to find mode for
        v_active 2560 h_active 1600 fps 60 pclk 278450

Both comparison sites are therefore taught the same derivation. For a non-DSC
panel the derived value equals pixel_clk_khz exactly, so those paths are
unchanged; only DSC panels, which are the broken case, see any difference.

Checked while writing this: nothing in techpack/display/msm/sde/ reads
mode->clock, so SDE clock budgeting is not affected by the new value.

--- a/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_defs.h
+++ b/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_defs.h
@@ -809,10 +809,36 @@
 static inline u64 dsi_h_total_dce(struct dsi_mode_info *mode)
 {
 	u64 h_total = dsi_h_active_dce(mode);
 
 	h_total += mode->h_back_porch + mode->h_front_porch +
 			mode->h_sync_width;
 	return h_total;
 }
 
+/*
+ * The pixel clock a DRM client must be shown for this mode. DRM defines
+ *	refresh = clock * 1000 / (htotal * vtotal)
+ * so the advertised clock has to be derived from the advertised (i.e.
+ * UNCOMPRESSED) timings or every client computes a bogus refresh rate.
+ *
+ * This deliberately differs from dsi_display_mode.pixel_clk_khz on a DSC
+ * panel: that one is computed over the COMPRESSED line width because it
+ * describes the DSI link, and it stays the value used to program the
+ * hardware. On a non-DSC panel the two are identical.
+ */
+static inline u32 dsi_drm_visible_clk_khz(const struct dsi_mode_info *t)
+{
+	u32 htot, vtot;
+
+	if (!t || !t->refresh_rate)
+		return 0;
+
+	htot = DSI_H_TOTAL(t);
+	vtot = DSI_V_TOTAL(t);
+	if (!htot || !vtot)
+		return 0;
+
+	return (htot * vtot * t->refresh_rate) / 1000;
+}
+
 #endif /* _DSI_DEFS_H_ */
--- a/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_drm.c
+++ b/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_drm.c
@@ -122,6 +122,16 @@
 	drm_mode->vtotal = drm_mode->vsync_end + dsi_mode->timing.v_back_porch;
 
 	drm_mode->vrefresh = dsi_mode->timing.refresh_rate;
-	drm_mode->clock = dsi_mode->pixel_clk_khz;
+	/*
+	 * NOT pixel_clk_khz: on a DSC panel that is the COMPRESSED link
+	 * rate, while the timings written above are uncompressed, and DRM
+	 * derives refresh from clock/(htotal*vtotal). This panel advertised
+	 * 112346 kHz against 1787x2597, so userspace drove the 60 Hz display
+	 * at 24.2 fps. pixel_clk_khz itself is untouched and still programs
+	 * the hardware; the two match-by-clock sites accept this form too.
+	 */
+	drm_mode->clock = dsi_drm_visible_clk_khz(&dsi_mode->timing);
+	if (!drm_mode->clock)
+		drm_mode->clock = dsi_mode->pixel_clk_khz;
 
 	drm_mode->private = (int *)dsi_mode->priv_info;
--- a/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_drm.c
+++ b/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_drm.c
@@ -641,8 +641,11 @@
 	for (i = 0; i < count; i++) {
 		dsi_mode = &dsi_display->modes[i];
 		if ((dsi_mode->timing.v_active == drm_mode->vdisplay) &&
 		    (dsi_mode->timing.h_active == drm_mode->hdisplay) &&
-		    (dsi_mode->pixel_clk_khz == drm_mode->clock) &&
+		    /* drm_mode->clock is the timings-derived form now */
+		    ((dsi_mode->pixel_clk_khz == drm_mode->clock) ||
+		     (dsi_drm_visible_clk_khz(&dsi_mode->timing) ==
+						drm_mode->clock)) &&
 		    (dsi_mode->timing.refresh_rate == drm_mode->vrefresh)) {
 			bit_clk_rate = dsi_mode->timing.clk_rate_hz;
 			break;
--- a/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_display.c
+++ b/techpack/display/msm/dsi/dsi_display.c
@@ -7618,3 +7618,11 @@
 			cmp->panel_mode == m->panel_mode &&
-			cmp->pixel_clk_khz == m->pixel_clk_khz) {
+			/*
+			 * cmp came from a drm mode, so its clock is the
